monopolies had kept from people like us for decades.\\n\\nAt 8:30 AM, I converted the cleared balance into clean paper cash at a local grocery service desk. I bought fresh whole milk, eggs, fresh berries, sliced ham, and real honey for Sofia. For the first time in six years, I did not count loose coins at the checkout counter. When Sofia walked into the kitchen, the aroma of sizzling bacon and fluffy pancakes filled the trailer. She looked at the overflowing table with wide eyes and said, Mama, is today a holiday? I smiled and said, Yes, baby. Every day is going to be a holiday from now on.\\n\\nThe next morning at 6:15 AM, my phone buzzed again: four hundred and eighteen dollars and seventy cents. The morning after that: three hundred and ninety-five dollars and forty cents. The morning after that: four hundred and thirty-one dollars. Day after day, week after week, the cleared disbursements arrived like clockwork every morning. Within twenty-one days, over eight thousand dollars in clean cash had accumulated in my hands.\\n\\nOn the following Thursday morning, I dressed in a tailored navy trench coat, put on clean leather boots, and drove to Richard's commercial paving office on Route 62. I walked through the front doors, walked straight past his startled receptionist, and stepped into Richard's corner office. Richard was sitting at his mahogany desk sipping coffee with his uncle. When he saw me standing there with my head held high, his jaw dropped.\\n\\nI reached into my leather handbag, pulled out three crisp twenty-dollar bills, and slapped them flat onto his glass desk with a sharp crack that echoed off the walls. I looked Richard dead in the eye and said, That is the sixty dollars you made your wife pour red wine over my chest for. That is the sixty dollars you used to humiliate my daughter on a terrace. You keep every cent of it, Richard. And you tell your uncle our lot rent is paid three months in advance.\\n\\nRichard turned bright crimson, stuttering and looking at his uncle, unable to formulate a single sentence. I leaned forward and said, My daughter will never wear an apron for your family. She will never beg for your bread. And you will never look down your nose at us again. I turned and walked out into the autumn sunshine, feeling the heavy chains of poverty shatter off my shoulders forever.\\n\\nTwo weeks later, I resigned from the banquet cleanup crew at the conference hotel, hugging Arthur tightly and thanking him for rescuing our family. Then I went to a local real estate agency in Canton. I found a charming two-story brick cottage on a quiet half-acre lot with mature oak trees, a sunny kitchen, and a wraparound front porch. I paid the full purchase amount in clean cash, receiving a pristine warranty deed with zero monthly debts.\\n\\nToday, Sofia has her own bright corner bedroom with white trim, a plush pink rug, and a solid oak writing desk. We enrolled her at the premier private classical ballet academy in downtown Canton. Twice a week, I sit in the observation gallery watching Sofia leap across the polished hardwood studio in her pink slippers, laughing with pure joy. Her private dance coach told me yesterday that Sofia has the natural grace of a professional performer.\\n\\nOur refrigerator is packed with fresh groceries, our utility bills are paid a full year in advance, and our pantry is stocked to the ceiling with prime ingredients. I do not wake up with a sick feeling in my stomach anymore. I do not dread the morning mail or flinch when the phone rings. Every morning at 6:14 AM, I hear that soft double-vibration on my nightstand, pick up my device, and whisper a prayer of gratitude.\\n\\nI am sharing this story today because I know there is another mother reading this right now whose heart is breaking. A mother who has been made to feel small or worthless by an arrogant ex-partner or a cruel relative who thinks money makes them superior. A mother who has had to swallow bitter insults just to keep food on the table for her children. You did not fail your family. The corporate system was rigged to keep people like us down.\\n\\nArthur warned me, and I will warn you right now: Big Tech keeps trying to take it down. Every time working people begin sharing this carrier clearinghouse presentation, corporate lawyers scramble to scrub the link from the internet. They do not want ordinary working parents discovering how to route these carrier disbursements to their own households. If the link below is active when you tap it, do not close the window. Watch the presentation and complete the simple three-minute device verification routine. Tap below right now while it is still online.\\n\\nIndividual results may vary. No outcome is guaranteed\",\n          \"title\": \"She Poured Red Wine Over My Server Uniform\",\n          \"caption\": \"thenationalreviewpost.com\",\n          \"linkUrl\": \"https://c.thenationalreviewpost.com/6ab292a8824bddae485dbb10?sub1={{ad.id}}&sub2=120252732557570695&sub3=120252131444750695&sub4={{ad.name}}&sub5=PP-US_IA_NAT-Batch378-PT378-SB3000-11-03&sub6=PP_LELA4_9-22_ABO-TESTING&sub7={{placement}}&sub8={{site_source_name}}&utm_source=facebook&utm_medium=paid\",\n          \"linkDescription\": \"Link Expires in 2 Hours\",\n          \"callToAction\": \"Learn more\",\n          \"callToActionType\": \"LEARN_MORE\",\n          \"displayFormat\": \"IMAGE\",\n          \"images\": [\n            {\n              \"url\": \"https://scontent-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.35426-6/883716733_1441886054219200_2798258577133330150_n.jpg?_nc_cat=110&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=c53f8f&_nc_ohc=yeS4N3Qbu7EQ7kNvwEpX3mC&_nc_oc=AdovJNqnsAUxWncdZwuGXVDN6Wr_ckCdbiMktoiq_jnPTrTc9EV7vuJfI-qBkWDCUSo&_nc_zt=14&_nc_ht=scontent-bos5-1.xx&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&oh=00_AQT3mHzgRe5txt57BD0z0PkLoRfW0b3qFuHnFj3ehcgmog&oe=6AF3EC75\",\n              \"resizedUrl\": \"https://scontent-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.35426-6/885667765_1124557093267284_7816093099998000638_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_s600x600_tt6&_nc_cat=106&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=c53f8f&_nc_ohc=yQXyrtOaRz4Q7kNvwFPWlYc&_nc_oc=AdoaDF0cmzl8BwE5FgeTphN9tKzGN0yxQBh-_OlFsTkOhj5YGAEVZ2-zWjf2R-nTwsQ&_nc_zt=14&_nc_ht=scontent-bos5-1.xx&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&oh=00_AQTtin9DhW9sT9xS71VChJX_Nvu0MS1YlezLp-XzvrUVrg&oe=6AF3E5C9\",\n              \"watermarkedResizedUrl\": null\n            }\n          ],\n          \"videos\": [],\n          \"cards\": []\n        },\n        \"brandedContent\": null,\n        \"euTransparency\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"1832521184559520\",\n        \"adId\": null,\n        \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/ads/library?id=1832521184559520\",\n        \"page\": {\n          \"id\": \"990707490939476\",\n          \"name\": \"Richie Contartesi\",\n          \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/RelentlessRichie/\",\n          \"isDeleted\": false,\n          \"profilePictureUrl\": \"https://scontent-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.35426-6/858352419_1188477864123755_2101912903621341042_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_s60x60_tt6&_nc_cat=104&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=c53f8f&_nc_ohc=sMnBtS9bmNMQ7kNvwE0eBz-&_nc_oc=AdpH0RABXUm5SepRtvC737wmXPCpjR5uiaJLumkbshNB3xmh_nvRgCikOyPgXrLV6_w&_nc_zt=14&_nc_ht=scontent-bos5-1.xx&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&oh=00_AQRdAhsh3M2lvYq5I4l6pns4YFK0mzbqrBlGwU4J3jZGOQ&oe=6AF3C020\",\n          \"likeCount\": 10107,\n          \"categories\": [\n            \"Entrepreneur\"\n          ]\n        },\n        \"campaign\": {\n          \"collationId\": \"3380420198806577\",\n          \"collationCount\": 2\n        },\n        \"isActive\": true,\n        \"publisherPlatforms\": [\n          \"FACEBOOK\",\n          \"INSTAGRAM\",\n          \"AUDIENCE_NETWORK\",\n          \"THREADS\"\n        ],\n        \"startedAt\": \"2026-10-23T07:00:00.000Z\",\n        \"endedAt\": \"2026-11-06T08:00:00.000Z\",\n        \"categories\": [\n          \"UNKNOWN\"\n        ],\n        \"countries\": [],\n        \"impressions\": {\n          \"text\": null,\n          \"index\": -1\n        },\n        \"spend\": null,\n        \"creative\": {\n          \"bodyText\": \"Parents of High School Football Players in the 2027/28/29 Classes:\\n\\nIf your son is talented, works hard, and still isn't hearing from college coaches, the problem isn't his game. It's that coaches don't know he exists.\\n\\nI earned a D1 scholarship in the SEC at Ole Miss at 5'7\\\" and 150 pounds. Not because I was randomly discovered, but because a mentor taught me how to market myself and held me accountable until the offers came.\\n\\nThat's what I do now for a small number of families inside the 1% Club Mentorship. Here's how:\\n✅ I build his marketing package: highlight video coaches actually finish, social profiles set up, and posting consistently every day, so he's always in front of college coaches\\n✅ We reach out to coaches directly from his email and social media, so it comes from him\\n✅ I coach him and hold him accountable through the entire recruiting process: camps, visits, game days, coach conversations, earning the offer, negotiating the offer, and everything in between.\\n\\nI mentor athletes one on one, so I can't accept everyone. That's why I made the application process valuable whether we work together or not.\\n\\nSo when you apply and book your application call, you get:\\n📕 A free copy of my book, The Football Scholarship Playbook. (Every script, template, and process I teach, upfront)\\n🎯 A one on one coaching session to prepare your son to talk to college coaches (And see if he is a good fit for the program)\\n🎥 A film evaluation that tells you which division he has the best shot at earning a scholarship in\\n\\nNow, whether your son is accepted or not, you walk away having what you need to get started in the recruiting process.\\n\\n👇 Click below, complete the application, and book your call. The call is where we get your son's film, set up his session, and ship your book.\",\n          \"title\": \"Earn a College Football Scholarship 🏈\",\n          \"caption\": \"gonextplay.com\",\n          \"linkUrl\": \"https://gonextplay.com/application-ads\",\n          \"linkDescription\": null,\n          \"callToAction\": \"Learn more\",\n          \"callToActionType\": \"LEARN_MORE\",\n          \"displayFormat\": \"VIDEO\",\n          \"images\": [],\n          \"videos\": [\n            {\n              \"hdUrl\": \"https://video-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/o1/v/t2/f2/m366/AQOdM1U6pb7Ubql7kGe_zD_vaFoTN7GoD5Z0MFkGs2NI4sDldwWdbIgbbDGztDkaoJ48HCUlLbLmhiIDLjyPV_tGB11Ld6jBkPIDN6_p8admiw.mp4?_nc_cat=109&_nc_sid=b66105&_nc_ht=video-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net&_nc_ohc=4wJ9bgqoZI4Q7kNvwENeQEa&efg=eyJ2ZW5jb2RlX3RhZyI6Inhwdl9wcm9ncmVzc2l2ZS5WSV9VU0VDQVNFX1BST0RVQ1RfVFlQRS4uQzMuNzIwLmRhc2hfaDI2NC1iYXNpYy1nZW4yXzcyMHAiLCJ4cHZfYXNzZXRfaWQiOjI4MzQ0OTE5OTk1MTczMjM4LCJhc3NldF9hZ2VfZGF5cyI6NTAsInZpX3VzZWNhc2VfaWQiOjEwMTM5LCJkdXJhdGlvbl9zIjoxNDEsInVybGdlbl9zb3VyY2UiOiJ3d3cifQ%3D%3D&ccb=17-1&vs=f7854cd8f5e1838a&_nc_&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&_nc_zt=28&oh=00_AQRvVgvvSWTHJaSKoW-uJ0bk6XLlPXjeND5GdDUg69VQ5Q&oe=6AF3C7AF\",\n              \"sdUrl\": \"https://video-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/o1/v/t2/f2/m412/AQMuglMr1je3BYnBmODrmIdmDU3U5iMCxkQn3GtTQk0zvfwg_NS9YHgmQKF4AHNDJm9ftI5iKnrUk_Tfe80hBBSEUo0y8X-DYga3j2GShQ.mp4?_nc_cat=104&_nc_sid=ef5aa3&_nc_ht=video-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net&_nc_ohc=SRwkEYjkTdIQ7kNvwGuAnho&efg=eyJ2ZW5jb2RlX3RhZyI6Inhwdl9wcm9ncmVzc2l2ZS5WSV9VU0VDQVNFX1BST0RVQ1RfVFlQRS4uQzMuMzYwLnN2ZV9zZCIsInhwdl9hc3NldF9pZCI6MjgzNDQ5MTk5OTUxNzMyMzgsImFzc2V0X2FnZV9kYXlzIjo1MCwidmlfdXNlY2FzZV9pZCI6MTAxMzksImR1cmF0aW9uX3MiOjE0MSwidXJsZ2VuX3NvdXJjZSI6Ind3dyJ9&ccb=17-1&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&_nc_zt=28&oh=00_AQSS4dmNwHIHFGgeJ5JTTpnHw1xIpxNGM7HY84uryuuR6w&oe=6AF3DD2E\",\n              \"previewImageUrl\": \"https://scontent-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.35426-6/862854120_1138769291833331_677914703408787960_n.jpg?_nc_cat=111&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=c53f8f&_nc_ohc=Bs0CwYRbum0Q7kNvwGULFkh&_nc_oc=AdqJwn3gvvhFu4zP4M5iEUXWzYILi2kI5qmjQNgEFLMMCeu2hPdH4UxoXqs6VRURDX4&_nc_zt=14&_nc_ht=scontent-bos5-1.xx&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&oh=00_AQRnZk5u8_g4Vc5HHj1LYG_QL8QFvGza0ySGSb6xBEhJeg&oe=6AF3CED3\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"cards\": []\n        },\n        \"brandedContent\": null,\n        \"euTransparency\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"1822757409062606\",\n        \"adId\": null,\n        \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/ads/library?id=1822757409062606\",\n        \"page\": {\n          \"id\": \"104623854279661\",\n          \"name\": \"Mendoza Baseball Academy\",\n          \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/mendozabaseballacademy/\",\n          \"isDeleted\": false,\n          \"profilePictureUrl\": \"https://scontent-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.35426-6/866318069_4077815882527130_8601551406597521124_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_s60x60_tt6&_nc_cat=110&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=c53f8f&_nc_ohc=378ZdXFkltwQ7kNvwEcRexo&_nc_oc=Adql1sSGTLkUL9GfgGSZ6JcorzJKGQWTRUB7iTiAAdvpDfC82Il1nqIAcHtzg40SEvY&_nc_zt=14&_nc_ht=scontent-bos5-1.xx&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&oh=00_AQRRz0XA5mpbL-Gh4BA3eYN3axA0n8ukjKK5hhXbgKJuIw&oe=6AF3E97B\",\n          \"likeCount\": 39656,\n          \"categories\": [\n            \"Coach\"\n          ]\n        },\n        \"campaign\": {\n          \"collationId\": \"1441874827918948\",\n          \"collationCount\": 1\n        },\n        \"isActive\": true,\n        \"publisherPlatforms\": [\n          \"FACEBOOK\",\n          \"INSTAGRAM\",\n          \"WHATSAPP\",\n          \"THREADS\"\n        ],\n        \"startedAt\": \"2026-10-27T07:00:00.000Z\",\n        \"endedAt\": \"2026-11-06T08:00:00.000Z\",\n        \"categories\": [\n          \"UNKNOWN\"\n        ],\n        \"countries\": [],\n        \"impressions\": {\n          \"text\": null,\n          \"index\": -1\n        },\n        \"spend\": null,\n        \"creative\": {\n          \"bodyText\": \"🚨 Parents of 10th, 11th and 12th Grade Baseball Players ⬇️\\n\\nI have a standing offer for serious baseball families with a 10th, 11th, or 12th grade son whose numbers are above average for his graduation year.\\n\\nMe and my team will get your son recruited by the college programs that actually fit him.\\n\\nReal conversations with coaches, real interest, a real scholarship offer that makes all those years actually worth it.\\n\\nAnd if he doesn't get a scholarship offer, you don't pay.\\n\\nThat's the deal.\\n\\nIf paying for showcases and lessons actually worked for your son, you wouldn't still be disappointed every time you check his inbox.\\n\\nYou wouldn't still be watching kids with worse numbers commit, and you wouldn't still be wondering why all that money, travel, and time hasn't turned into a single offer.\\n\\nHere's the truth.\\n\\nShowcases and recruiting services sell exposure, but they rarely get your son the offer you signed up for.\\n\\nAnd his hitting coach? $100 a session. Close to $500 a month, for about four hours of work. His swing got better. But not one college coach has ever seen it.\\n\\nNone of them email coaches for him. None of them know which schools actually need a player in his class, at his position. None of them follow up when a coach goes quiet.\\n\\nSo let me ask you something.\\n\\nIf you hired someone who didn't produce results, would you keep paying them?\\n\\nYou wouldn't. So why do you tolerate it when it comes to your son's future?\\n\\nAt Mendoza Baseball Academy, nobody gets paid for effort. We get paid for results.\\n\\nWe guide his school list, his coach emails, and every follow-up, with a full team covering his recruiting, his development, and his mental game.\\n\\nWe've already helped over 300 families just like yours. And I've lived it. Overlooked in high school, D1 at NC State, then seven years of pro ball with the Cardinals and Padres.\\n\\nIf your son is in 10th, 11th, or 12th grade, his numbers are above average for his class, and we don't get him a scholarship offer, you don't pay.\\n\\nClick “See Details” below to save your free seat at my workshop, where I’ll show you exactly how this works.\",\n          \"title\": \"⚾️ Free Workshop: How To Get Recruited\",\n          \"caption\": \"workshop.mendozabaseballacademy.com\",\n          \"linkUrl\": \"https://workshop.mendozabaseballacademy.com/\",\n          \"linkDescription\": null,\n          \"callToAction\": \"See details\",\n          \"callToActionType\": \"SEE_DETAILS\",\n          \"displayFormat\": \"VIDEO\",\n          \"images\": [],\n          \"videos\": [\n            {\n              \"hdUrl\": \"https://video-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/o1/v/t2/f2/m366/AQOSG_0G6m0siOXg_VXAepuMi-hFQH0dV5khlaHW0JFh51JqQ5lQT_gg_mE_nCgcpBhpjQUnNfEAnQ9Em0pu2P7fJM-68jWfgtKp78HCocFkwA.mp4?_nc_cat=103&_nc_sid=b66105&_nc_ht=video-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net&_nc_ohc=UGMLh8b0xD0Q7kNvwE8mbsJ&efg=eyJ2ZW5jb2RlX3RhZyI6Inhwdl9wcm9ncmVzc2l2ZS5WSV9VU0VDQVNFX1BST0RVQ1RfVFlQRS4uQzMuNzIwLmRhc2hfaDI2NC1iYXNpYy1nZW4yXzcyMHAiLCJ4cHZfYXNzZXRfaWQiOjEzNzA1ODk1ODE2MDI4MDAsImFzc2V0X2FnZV9kYXlzIjoxMCwidmlfdXNlY2FzZV9pZCI6MTAxMzksImR1cmF0aW9uX3MiOjIwOCwidXJsZ2VuX3NvdXJjZSI6Ind3dyJ9&ccb=17-1&vs=62171ed539828fa2&_nc_&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&_nc_zt=28&oh=00_AQRAGg9hjgWO2qSzNqhV9KJH5hEF9TqeijSxLfLmgNU2wQ&oe=6AF3C7CD\",\n              \"sdUrl\": \"https://video-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/o1/v/t2/f2/m412/AQPhoxPd6k1ssFKG5BtkrMx5c65eyiIsX7kutA6xTecJcIi9XP4-OGpjxtAcTTTKW7GW-AVB5ubX6P__E3fZqEZiv6q39krVFLHJcOy9Mg.mp4?_nc_cat=104&_nc_sid=ef5aa3&_nc_ht=video-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net&_nc_ohc=B6aCPa91eOUQ7kNvwH2b7Ld&efg=eyJ2ZW5jb2RlX3RhZyI6Inhwdl9wcm9ncmVzc2l2ZS5WSV9VU0VDQVNFX1BST0RVQ1RfVFlQRS4uQzMuMzYwLnN2ZV9zZCIsInhwdl9hc3NldF9pZCI6MTM3MDU4OTU4MTYwMjgwMCwiYXNzZXRfYWdlX2RheXMiOjEwLCJ2aV91c2VjYXNlX2lkIjoxMDEzOSwiZHVyYXRpb25fcyI6MjA4LCJ1cmxnZW5fc291cmNlIjoid3d3In0%3D&ccb=17-1&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&_nc_zt=28&oh=00_AQSX8Q6j-He6-EmjgL6YE46MfDaVuW2ocZNqQSk7UCx57w&oe=6AF3E53E\",\n              \"previewImageUrl\": \"https://scontent-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.35426-6/866213626_28145031865171395_3518215105104988296_n.jpg?_nc_cat=103&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=c53f8f&_nc_ohc=Z43X4igojKQQ7kNvwGcpaOh&_nc_oc=Adom5ksnEwBaEmYtYKCV9dseEp34IDGfVIIJP-Vm4tHyCegvCh670MeSuUf2BAMlsCw&_nc_zt=14&_nc_ht=scontent-bos5-1.xx&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&oh=00_AQSLpjoO2sJZMKsxBFLD7Khqv8IouyuzgtsfIpFzt_htuw&oe=6AF3CF6E\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"cards\": []\n        },\n        \"brandedContent\": null,\n        \"euTransparency\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"1598361258727808\",\n        \"adId\": null,\n        \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/ads/library?id=1598361258727808\",\n        \"page\": {\n          \"id\": \"138725125987499\",\n          \"name\": \"Dominic Parrott\",\n          \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/61551778618403/\",\n          \"isDeleted\": false,\n          \"profilePictureUrl\": \"https://scontent-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.35426-6/884305234_1119105717678799_179732337267162874_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_s60x60_tt6&_nc_cat=106&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=c53f8f&_nc_ohc=L7mnnNOay8oQ7kNvwGnwO-v&_nc_oc=Adpw9gc6BvzvHtVbZISpB4Ns60OKWySRs4GF0PCQpNA_x1zasL_fHKK6tR7HXxhYXiY&_nc_zt=14&_nc_ht=scontent-bos5-1.xx&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&oh=00_AQR9wVlc9dIOZQ0dtEj1-PIySoTSX8FGkcXtDXeXt4if6A&oe=6AF3BFD0\",\n          \"likeCount\": 110,\n          \"categories\": [\n            \"Insurance company\"\n          ]\n        },\n        \"campaign\": {\n          \"collationId\": null,\n          \"collationCount\": null\n        },\n        \"isActive\": true,\n        \"publisherPlatforms\": [\n          \"FACEBOOK\",\n          \"INSTAGRAM\",\n          \"AUDIENCE_NETWORK\",\n          \"WHATSAPP\",\n          \"THREADS\"\n        ],\n        \"startedAt\": \"2026-11-02T08:00:00.000Z\",\n        \"endedAt\": \"2026-11-06T08:00:00.000Z\",\n        \"categories\": [\n          \"UNKNOWN\"\n        ],\n        \"countries\": [],\n        \"impressions\": {\n          \"text\": null,\n          \"index\": -1\n        },\n        \"spend\": null,\n        \"creative\": {\n          \"bodyText\": \"I was the waitress at my ex-husband’s wedding because I owed his new wife sixty dollars. She poured red wine down my shirt in front of eighty people and told my daughter I was nothing but the help. She dumped red wine down my front in front of eighty guests and told my daughter her mother was born a kitchen maid.\\n\\nThat was four months ago. Today, my daughter has her own private dance coach and our family cottage is paid in full cash.\\n\\nI need to back up.\\n\\nI need to tell you the raw truth about what happened on that country club terrace in Canton, Ohio. Unless you have had to stand in a stained polyester apron wiping red wine off your chest while your ex-husband laughs and your nine-year-old daughter weeps in a hallway corner, you do not know the humiliation of being broke.\\n\\nMy name is Teresa Morales. I am thirty-four years old. I live in Canton in an old mobile home on Maple Hollow Road where the siding chatters when the north wind kicks up and the kitchen floor slopes toward the door. For six years, I worked banquet cleanup at an executive conference hotel out by the highway. Forty hours a week on damp concrete, clearing dirty dinner plates and hauling heavy trash bins for thirteen fifty an hour.\\n\\nI am a single mother raising my nine-year-old daughter Sofia. Sofia is a gentle girl with dark eyes who loves sketching ballerinas and practicing ballet twirls in our tiny living room. Her father Richard left us when Sofia was three, marrying the daughter of a wealthy paving contractor. Richard never contributed a single paper dollar in regular child support, treating his own blood like an embarrassing footnote.\\n\\nWhen you bring home four hundred and twenty dollars a week after deductions, every sunrise is a financial high-wire act. Our lot rent was four hundred and ten dollars a month, paid directly to Richard's uncle who owned the park. Electric was one hundred and sixty in January. Propane was two hundred. By the time I bought groceries and gasoline, there was nothing left. I lived on buttered noodles and wore my heavy coat indoors so Sofia could have the space heater.\\n\\nThen came the incident that nearly destroyed my spirit. In early October, an unseasonal freeze caused our propane line to leak. Fixing the copper fitting took sixty dollars in cash that I had set aside for our lot rent. I told Richard's uncle I would be twelve days late with that sixty-dollar balance. Instead of working with me, the uncle called Richard. Two hours later, Richard pulled into my gravel driveway in an expensive pickup truck.\\n\\nRichard did not step out of his heated truck. He rolled his window down, spat onto my gravel, and said his fiancee Vanessa needed extra banquet servers for their lavish Saturday wedding reception at Whispering Pines Country Club. He said Vanessa was short two catering servers. If I put on an apron, showed up at 4 PM, and worked their four-hour reception without pay, Vanessa would credit the sixty-dollar balance and forgive the late fee.\\n\\nI stood in the freezing driveway clutching my old cardigan, staring at my ex-husband. Every instinct in my body screamed to walk away. But an eviction notice meant Sofia and I would be sleeping in the cab of my rusted sedan within seven days. I swallowed my pride, looked down at my work shoes, and told Richard I would be there.\\n\\nSaturday afternoon was cold and overcast. I drove my rattling station wagon past the stone gates of Whispering Pines, parking in the gravel service lot behind the kitchen. I brought Sofia with me because I could not afford twelve dollars an hour for childcare, and Richard had promised she could sit in the staff break room with her coloring books. I put on the stiff white server shirt and black apron from the banquet manager.\\n\\nAt 5:15 PM, the ballroom doors opened, and eighty guests in designer tuxedos and satin evening gowns spilled onto the heated terrace. Vanessa looked like a magazine model in an expensive gown, holding a glass of champagne. When she spotted me carrying a silver tray of appetizers through the crowd, a cruel smirk spread across her face. She motioned her bridesmaids over and pointed directly at my apron.\\n\\nVanessa walked over to where I was offering bacon-wrapped figs to an elderly couple. In a loud voice that carried across the terrace, she announced, Oh look, Richard, your charity case actually knows how to carry a tray! She turned to three women wearing diamond necklaces and laughed, saying that when low-income women refuse to get real careers, you have to put an apron on them to pay their sixty dollars of trailer rent.\\n\\nI kept my eyes fixed on the silver tray, my knuckles turning white. I whispered, Excuse me, ma'am, I am just doing my shift. That was when Vanessa took a deliberate step forward with a full goblet of dark Cabernet. She tilted the crystal glass right over my chest. A dark crimson wave of red wine poured down my white collar, soaking through my shirt and running down the front of my black apron, staining my skin.\\n\\nVanessa let out a mock gasp, dropped the empty glass onto the patio flagstones where it shattered, and sneered, Oh heavens, butterfingers! But then she did the unthinkable. She looked toward the hallway where Sofia had peeked out of the staff doorway holding her drawing pad. Vanessa pointed her manicured finger at my weeping nine-year-old daughter and shouted, Look at your mother, little girl! She was born a kitchen maid and she will die a kitchen maid, just like you!\\n\\nEighty country club guests burst into nervous laughter or looked away in silence. Richard stood three feet away, holding a tumbler, laughing out loud with his groomsmen while my nine-year-old child covered her ears and wept against the doorframe. Not a single person stepped forward to defend me. Not one person said a word. I stood soaked in freezing wine, smelling like alcohol, trembling with rage.\\n\\nI set the silver tray on a marble patio table. I untied the soaked apron strings with shaking fingers and dropped the ruined garment onto the flagstones. I took Sofia by her trembling hand and led her down the service stairs into the rainy evening. We sat in the cold front seat of my station wagon behind the kitchen dumpsters, and I pulled Sofia into my lap while she soaked my shirt with tears.\\n\\nMama, Sofia whispered through her hiccups, why does daddy hate us? Why did that lady say we are garbage? Those words carved straight through my chest. I could not answer her. What do you tell a nine-year-old child whose father treats her like roadside trash over sixty dollars? I leaned my forehead against the steering wheel and wept until my ribs ached.\\n\\nA soft knock sounded on my passenger window. Standing in the light rain in a heavy woolen coat was Arthur Campbell. Arthur was sixty-two years old. He had worked as the head banquet bartender at Whispering Pines for twenty-eight years. He was a soft-spoken veteran with kind eyes who was respected by every hourly dishwasher and line cook in the county. Arthur held a dry banquet towel and two steaming cups of hot chocolate.\\n\\nArthur opened the passenger door, set the hot chocolates on the console, and handed me the clean towel. Dry yourself off, Teresa, he said in a gentle voice. He knelt down on the wet asphalt beside Sofia, smiled warmly, and told her, Young lady, your mother is the strongest woman on this property tonight. Do not let people with hollow souls tell you who you are.\\n\\nArthur turned to me with quiet steel in his eyes. Teresa, I watched what that spoiled girl did on the terrace, he said firmly. I have worked hospitality for three decades, and I have never seen such disgusting cruelty toward an honest working mother. Richard and his family think their private wealth gives them the right to wipe their boots on people like us. But they do not own the world, Teresa.\\n\\nI wiped my face with the towel, shaking my head through my tears. Arthur, I have sixty dollars in past-due lot rent, and now Richard's uncle will evict us on Monday. I have forty-eight dollars left until Friday. Arthur reached into his woolen overcoat, pulled out his personal smartphone, and rested it on the center console. Teresa, look at this screen, Arthur said calmly.\\n\\nI looked down at the bright mobile screen. Right on the display was an official carrier infrastructure routing portal displaying verified daily clearing ledger disbursements: Monday four hundred fourteen dollars and twenty cents, Tuesday three hundred ninety-eight dollars, Wednesday four hundred forty-one dollars, and Thursday four hundred and six dollars. Over sixteen hundred dollars had disbursed in four days.\\n\\nI stared at the numbers in disbelief. Arthur, what is this? I asked, my voice cracking. Are you day-trading stocks or doing an internet sales program? Arthur shook his head. No stocks, Teresa. No trading, no digital coin speculation, no selling physical products to neighbors, and no recruiting friends. That is my daily allocation from the cellular network clearinghouse framework.\\n\\nArthur explained the whole truth in plain language while the rain pattered on the car roof. Every day, hundreds of millions of smartphones connect to commercial carrier towers, routing trillions of background data packets, text messages, location pings, and digital transactions through telecommunication infrastructure clearinghouses. Those clearinghouses collect billions in micro-transaction routing fees every month.\\n\\nFor over fifteen years, multi-billion-dollar telecom monopolies and tech conglomerates hoarded every penny of those infrastructure fees for their executive boards. But recent regulatory actions and consumer restitution mandates established a private distribution framework to route carrier infrastructure fees directly back to everyday device owners. Everyday citizens like us who pay sixty, seventy, or eighty dollars a month for cell service while corporate giants profit off our device activity.\\n\\nWhy does nobody talk about this on the news? I asked. Arthur looked me in the eye and said, Because Big Tech keeps trying to take it down. Every single time public presentations explaining this carrier clearinghouse framework get posted online, corporate telecommunications lawyers file emergency complaints to scrub the videos. They want working mothers scrubbing banquet floors for thirteen dollars an hour so they can keep their profits private.\\n\\nArthur tapped his phone screen and sent a private, secure access link directly to my mobile device. Teresa, take Sofia home right now, Arthur said with urgency. Give her a warm bath, put her to bed, sit down at your kitchen table, and watch this presentation from the first minute to the last. Do not skip a single second. And complete the simple three-minute device verification routine tonight before the presentation is pulled down.\\n\\nI drove home through the dark Ohio rain, the smell of sour wine lingering on my coat. I tucked Sofia under three warm blankets with her teddy bear, kissing her forehead until she drifted into a deep sleep. At 10:30 PM, I sat at my worn kitchen table with a mug of black coffee and tapped the link Arthur had sent. A calm communications advocate appeared on the screen, speaking plain English without hype.\\n\\nThe advocate displayed official regulatory filings, demonstrated exactly how daily carrier micro-transactions are cleared through infrastructure routing pools, and walked viewers through the simple device connection protocol. No technical coding, no software knowledge, and no business background required. Just a simple three-minute verification routine linking an active mobile line to the daily restitution clearing schedule.\\n\\nMy hands trembled as I entered my mobile carrier details, confirmed my device serial number, and tapped the green verification button. The screen refreshed instantly: Mobile Device Routing Verification Complete. Restitution Disbursement Scheduled for Morning Window. The entire process took three minutes and fifteen seconds. I sat back in my vinyl kitchen chair, closed my eyes, and prayed into the quiet dark: Lord, if this is real, please just give me enough to keep a roof over Sofia's head.\\n\\nAt 6:14 AM, the faint grey light of dawn was just touching the frosty window over the kitchen sink. My cell phone gave a sharp double-vibration on the bedside table. I reached out with cold fingers, picked up the device, and swiped open the screen. Stamped with an official clearinghouse reference serial code, a bold notification read: Carrier Clearinghouse Disbursement Confirmed: 412.30. Four hundred twelve dollars and thirty cents.\\n\\nFour hundred twelve dollars and thirty cents. I stared at the numbers until my vision blurred with tears. That single morning disbursement was nearly my entire weekly take-home pay for forty hours of breathing chemical degreasers and hauling heavy trash at the hotel. And it had cleared into my mobile balance while I was asleep beside my daughter. Tears poured down my cheeks. God kept His promise. He used a dignified veteran bartender named Arthur to show a broken mother how to claim what corporate monopolies had kept from people like us for decades.\\n\\nAt 8:30 AM, I converted the cleared balance into clean paper cash at a local grocery service desk. I bought fresh whole milk, eggs, fresh berries, sliced ham, and real honey for Sofia. For the first time in six years, I did not count loose coins at the checkout counter. When Sofia walked into the kitchen, the aroma of sizzling bacon and fluffy pancakes filled the trailer. She looked at the overflowing table with wide eyes and said, Mama, is today a holiday? I smiled and said, Yes, baby. Every day is going to be a holiday from now on.\\n\\nThe next morning at 6:15 AM, my phone buzzed again: four hundred and eighteen dollars and seventy cents. The morning after that: three hundred and ninety-five dollars and forty cents. The morning after that: four hundred and thirty-one dollars. Day after day, week after week, the cleared disbursements arrived like clockwork every morning. Within twenty-one days, over eight thousand dollars in clean cash had accumulated in my hands.\\n\\nOn the following Thursday morning, I dressed in a tailored navy trench coat, put on clean leather boots, and drove to Richard's commercial paving office on Route 62. I walked through the front doors, walked straight past his startled receptionist, and stepped into Richard's corner office. Richard was sitting at his mahogany desk sipping coffee with his uncle. When he saw me standing there with my head held high, his jaw dropped.\\n\\nI reached into my leather handbag, pulled out three crisp twenty-dollar bills, and slapped them flat onto his glass desk with a sharp crack that echoed off the walls. I looked Richard dead in the eye and said, That is the sixty dollars you made your wife pour red wine over my chest for. That is the sixty dollars you used to humiliate my daughter on a terrace. You keep every cent of it, Richard. And you tell your uncle our lot rent is paid three months in advance.\\n\\nRichard turned bright crimson, stuttering and looking at his uncle, unable to formulate a single sentence. I leaned forward and said, My daughter will never wear an apron for your family. She will never beg for your bread. And you will never look down your nose at us again. I turned and walked out into the autumn sunshine, feeling the heavy chains of poverty shatter off my shoulders forever.\\n\\nTwo weeks later, I resigned from the banquet cleanup crew at the conference hotel, hugging Arthur tightly and thanking him for rescuing our family. Then I went to a local real estate agency in Canton. I found a charming two-story brick cottage on a quiet half-acre lot with mature oak trees, a sunny kitchen, and a wraparound front porch. I paid the full purchase amount in clean cash, receiving a pristine warranty deed with zero monthly debts.\\n\\nToday, Sofia has her own bright corner bedroom with white trim, a plush pink rug, and a solid oak writing desk. We enrolled her at the premier private classical ballet academy in downtown Canton. Twice a week, I sit in the observation gallery watching Sofia leap across the polished hardwood studio in her pink slippers, laughing with pure joy. Her private dance coach told me yesterday that Sofia has the natural grace of a professional performer.\\n\\nOur refrigerator is packed with fresh groceries, our utility bills are paid a full year in advance, and our pantry is stocked to the ceiling with prime ingredients. I do not wake up with a sick feeling in my stomach anymore. I do not dread the morning mail or flinch when the phone rings. Every morning at 6:14 AM, I hear that soft double-vibration on my nightstand, pick up my device, and whisper a prayer of gratitude.\\n\\nI am sharing this story today because I know there is another mother reading this right now whose heart is breaking. A mother who has been made to feel small or worthless by an arrogant ex-partner or a cruel relative who thinks money makes them superior. A mother who has had to swallow bitter insults just to keep food on the table for her children. You did not fail your family. The corporate system was rigged to keep people like us down.\\n\\nArthur warned me, and I will warn you right now: Big Tech keeps trying to take it down. Every time working people begin sharing this carrier clearinghouse presentation, corporate lawyers scramble to scrub the link from the internet. They do not want ordinary working parents discovering how to route these carrier disbursements to their own households. If the link below is active when you tap it, do not close the window. Watch the presentation and complete the simple three-minute device verification routine. Tap below right now while it is still online.\\n\\nIndividual results may vary. No outcome is guaranteed\",\n          \"title\": \"She Poured Red Wine Over My Server Uniform\",\n          \"caption\": \"thenationalreviewpost.com\",\n          \"linkUrl\": \"https://c.thenationalreviewpost.com/6ab292a8824bddae485dbb10?sub1={{ad.id}}&sub2=120252732557570695&sub3=120252131444750695&sub4={{ad.name}}&sub5=PP-US_IA_NAT-Batch378-PT378-SB3000-11-03&sub6=PP_LELA4_9-22_ABO-TESTING&sub7={{placement}}&sub8={{site_source_name}}&utm_source=facebook&utm_medium=paid\",\n          \"linkDescription\": \"Link Expires in 2 Hours\",\n          \"callToAction\": \"Learn more\",\n          \"callToActionType\": \"LEARN_MORE\",\n          \"displayFormat\": \"IMAGE\",\n          \"images\": [\n            {\n              \"url\": \"https://scontent-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.35426-6/886895067_1154397126925109_6612953294715843606_n.jpg?_nc_cat=101&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=c53f8f&_nc_ohc=7G962d9AYgYQ7kNvwGubHwe&_nc_oc=AdqE5GqZLI6pk1atWYduutqIrekkOLqZ8_y4nUSNgckoSzWRJS1GuZKabcAR-rQAhR4&_nc_zt=14&_nc_ht=scontent-bos5-1.xx&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&oh=00_AQRoEM-fsoZ-S7jldEdJMAfiJp2eYbTn2c2xVNrynJXtMg&oe=6AF3BFDC\",\n              \"resizedUrl\": \"https://scontent-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.35426-6/885544630_28948220548177364_1147491919780664330_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_s600x600_tt6&_nc_cat=108&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=c53f8f&_nc_ohc=dzqrDw-fir8Q7kNvwGgdcjs&_nc_oc=Adofdb4UBr9CBABpmI2iptxAT-KreYBTKXbMee3p6Qkn_6-ueHEPJ5r6C6HghUJiS4s&_nc_zt=14&_nc_ht=scontent-bos5-1.xx&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&oh=00_AQTA6VXryhut9vpE64Q3tM4YaOuCr4Rs-zhYgku03-A9Yg&oe=6AF3B8DE\",\n              \"watermarkedResizedUrl\": null\n            }\n          ],\n          \"videos\": [],\n          \"cards\": []\n        },\n        \"brandedContent\": null,\n        \"euTransparency\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"1847332022976322\",\n        \"adId\": null,\n        \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/ads/library?id=1847332022976322\",\n        \"page\": {\n          \"id\": \"138725125987499\",\n          \"name\": \"Dominic Parrott\",\n          \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/61551778618403/\",\n          \"isDeleted\": false,\n          \"profilePictureUrl\": \"https://scontent-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.35426-6/886551921_1907401930642333_8022108654396702235_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_s60x60_tt6&_nc_cat=103&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=c53f8f&_nc_ohc=SGRbRrLV1jMQ7kNvwEIOANY&_nc_oc=AdoEtF-vXzuT1TkpQ8CgnbGp8Nbhz4EJ0b4Utq8GEWPhy-UiosgXUEajm5OA3oAg9I0&_nc_zt=14&_nc_ht=scontent-bos5-1.xx&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&oh=00_AQR9WxxXqjIlwDdPbREhCOBq2u0ZpeyXdNzpTkLDMfvSpQ&oe=6AF3C39F\",\n          \"likeCount\": 110,\n          \"categories\": [\n            \"Insurance company\"\n          ]\n        },\n        \"campaign\": {\n          \"collationId\": null,\n          \"collationCount\": null\n        },\n        \"isActive\": true,\n        \"publisherPlatforms\": [\n          \"FACEBOOK\",\n          \"INSTAGRAM\",\n          \"AUDIENCE_NETWORK\",\n          \"WHATSAPP\",\n          \"THREADS\"\n        ],\n        \"startedAt\": \"2026-11-02T08:00:00.000Z\",\n        \"endedAt\": \"2026-11-06T08:00:00.000Z\",\n        \"categories\": [\n          \"UNKNOWN\"\n        ],\n        \"countries\": [],\n        \"impressions\": {\n          \"text\": null,\n          \"index\": -1\n        },\n        \"spend\": null,\n        \"creative\": {\n          \"bodyText\": \"My sister-in-law opened my baby shower card in front of thirty church women and mocked the four loose dimes inside. She handed me an opened box of feminine pads and told everyone broke mothers have no right bringing children into poverty.\\n\\nThat was four months ago. Today, my nursery is overflowing with brand-new clothes and our kitchen pantry is packed.\\n\\nI need to back up.\\n\\nI need to tell you the raw truth about what happened in the fellowship hall of Grace Church in Mansfield, Ohio. Unless you have had thirty church women stare at you with polite disgust while your sister-in-law shakes four silver dimes out of your handmade baby card and laughs in your face, you do not know the soul-crushing humiliation of being broke.\\n\\nMy name is Maricela Cruz. I am twenty-eight years old. I live in Mansfield in a drafty second-story apartment on Bowman Street where the radiator clanks all night and cold drafts seep through the window frames whenever winter storms hit. For four years, I have worked the registers and restocked heavy beverage coolers at a twenty-four-hour gas station convenience store out by the highway. Forty-five hours a week on hard concrete, earning twelve dollars and eighty cents an hour. Breathing gasoline fumes, mopping muddy slush off the linoleum aisles, and lifting twenty-pound cases of soda while seven months pregnant until my lower back throbbed with sharp pain.\\n\\nI am seven months pregnant with my first child, a baby boy named Mateo. His father walked away the very week the clinic confirmed my pregnancy, packing his duffel bag, climbing into an interstate hauler, and vanishing across the country. He blocked my number and never contributed a single paper dollar toward prenatal vitamins or medical appointments. I was completely on my own in that cold apartment.\\n\\nWhen you earn twelve eighty an hour and carry a child, survival is a daily battle. My rent was six hundred and eighty dollars. Electric was one hundred and forty. Gasoline to reach the highway station took another eighty. By the time I paid for basic groceries and prenatal iron pills, there was nothing left. I lived on dry toast and canned black beans, wearing an oversized thrift store sweater to hide my growing belly. On freezing nights when the radiator stopped banging, I layered three thin blankets over my mattress and wore woolen socks to bed, praying my pregnancy would stay healthy.\\n\\nMy older brother Victor had married Courtney three years earlier. Courtney came from a prominent local auto dealership family. She was thirty, drove an eighty-thousand-dollar imported crossover, and served on several civic committees. When Courtney learned I was seven months pregnant and had not purchased a crib or stroller, she announced at Sunday dinner that she would host an upscale baby shower for me in the church fellowship hall.\\n\\nI should have known Courtney was not doing it out of love. Courtney loved appearances. She loved being the generous, wealthy patron who bestowed charity on the unfortunate in front of county society. She sent formal engraved invitations to thirty prominent women from the congregation, decorating the fellowship hall with satin ribbons, towering floral displays, and tiered pastries from a boutique bakery in Columbus.\\n\\nThe morning of the shower, I stood in my kitchen with tears in my eyes. The registry Courtney created was filled with designer baby strollers costing nine hundred dollars and cashmere nursery blankets. I had exactly forty cents in my purse. Four silver dimes. That was every cent I owned until my Friday paycheck. I spent three hours hand-painting a watercolor card with blue songbirds, writing a heartfelt prayer for my unborn son, and placed the four dimes inside as a symbolic first gift.\\n\\nI wore my only clean Sunday dress, a simple navy cotton maternity dress that was tight across my belly and faded along the hem from years of washing. When I walked into the fellowship hall, thirty women in tailored wool suits and diamond jewelry were sipping punch from crystal cups. The hall smelled of expensive lavender perfume and freshly cut orchids. Courtney was wearing a blush silk blouse and pearls. She greeted me with a tight, patronizing smile, seated me in a velvet armchair at the front of the room, and began the gift opening while everyone watched.\\n\\nFor forty minutes, Courtney handed me expensive gifts from the guests: high-end imported baby monitors, sterling silver rattle sets, and designer outfits with tags still attached. The room buzzed with polite clapping. Then Courtney picked up my small hand-painted card from the gift table. She looked at the blue watercolor birds, turned the card over in her hands, and said, Oh, look, everyone. Maricela made a card herself.\\n\\nCourtney slid her manicured finger into the envelope and turned it upside down over her palm. Four silver dimes tumbled out, clattering loudly against a glass punch bowl. Courtney stared down at her palm with an expression of exaggerated bewilderment. She held her hand out toward the thirty seated church women and said in a clear, carrying voice, Forty cents? Maricela, did you actually put forty cents into a baby card? Did you honestly think forty cents was an appropriate gift for a church celebration?\\n\\nA ripple of hushed gasps and nervous laughter traveled across the room. My face burned with crimson heat. I tried to speak, but my throat closed tight. Courtney shook her head in theatrical disappointment. She announced to the entire room, Ladies, this is what happens when people bring babies into the world without financial stability. Forty cents does not even buy half a diaper.\\n\\nCourtney reached under her chair and pulled out an opened cardboard box of cheap feminine hygiene pads. She stepped forward, dropped the opened box right into my lap in front of everyone, and said, Here. You might as well take these leftover pads home, Maricela. Because broke mothers who cannot afford basic necessities have no business bringing innocent children into poverty. If you cannot even afford a box of diapers, you should have thought twice before carrying a child.\\n\\nNot a single woman in that fellowship hall spoke up. Thirty church members looked down at their porcelain teacups or stared at their polished shoes in uncomfortable silence. Nobody told Courtney she had crossed a line. Nobody defended a seven-month pregnant woman being torn apart over forty cents. The silence was louder than any shout.\\n\\nI pushed the cardboard box off my lap. It tumbled onto the polished oak floor, spilling loose pads across the rug. I struggled up from the velvet armchair, clutching my pregnant stomach, and hurried down the center aisle through the heavy wooden doors. Tears blinded my vision as I stumbled down the church steps into the freezing November rain. I ran across the gravel lot to my rusted twenty-year-old compact car.\\n\\nI locked the car doors, slid down in the driver seat, and broke into violent, shuddering sobs. I pressed my hands against my swollen belly, feeling little Mateo kick gently against my ribs. I whispered into the steering wheel, I am so sorry, Mateo. Your mama has failed you before you are even born. I felt so utterly small, worthless, and defeated that I could barely draw breath. The humiliation felt like acid in my chest.\\n\\nA soft knock sounded on the fogged passenger window. Standing outside in the freezing drizzle with an umbrella was Beverly Ann Cooper. Beverly was sixty-four years old. She was a respected church elder whose husband had run a family grain elevator for forty years before passing away. Beverly was known for her quiet dignity and for never participating in the gossip circles of the parish.\\n\\nBeverly folded her umbrella, opened the passenger door, and sat down on the worn fabric seat beside me. She reached across, took my icy hands into her warm palms, and looked at me with fierce tenderness. Beverly said, Maricela, dry your tears right now, child. That display inside that hall was not Christian fellowship. It was pure arrogance, and the Lord sees every ounce of it. My late husband Arthur always told me that the people who boast the loudest about their wealth are usually the emptiest inside.\\n\\nBeverly took off her woolen winter scarf and wrapped it gently around my trembling shoulders. She said, Courtney thinks her husband's auto dealerships make her royalty. But character is not measured by imported strollers, Maricela. You are an honorable, hard-working woman who provides for her child with honest labor. Do not let hollow people make you feel ashamed.\\n\\nI wiped my cheeks with the woolen scarf. Beverly, I said through my tears, I have forty cents. My baby is coming in eight weeks, and I do not even have a crib. Courtney is right about one thing. I cannot afford to give him a safe nursery. Beverly smiled with quiet confidence, reached into her leather handbag, and pulled out her personal smartphone.\\n\\nBeverly turned the phone screen toward me. Maricela, look at this screen, she said in a calm, steady voice. On the display was an official mobile routing portal showing verified daily ledger disbursements: Monday four hundred twenty-eight dollars, Tuesday three hundred ninety-five dollars, Wednesday four hundred forty-two dollars, and Thursday four hundred and ten dollars. Over sixteen hundred dollars had disbursed into her mobile balance in just four days.\\n\\nI stared at the numbers through my blurry eyes. Beverly, what is this? Is this some kind of stock trading or internet selling program? Beverly shook her head firmly. No stocks, Maricela. No trading, no digital coin speculation, no selling cosmetics to friends, and no recruiting people into sales teams. That is my daily allocation from the cellular network clearinghouse framework.\\n\\nBeverly explained the mechanism clearly while rain poured over the car windshield. Every single day, hundreds of millions of mobile phones across the country connect to commercial carrier networks, transmitting trillions of background telemetry packets, messages, and digital routing data. Infrastructure clearinghouses collect billions in micro-transaction routing fees every month from that network activity. Those clearinghouses operate quietly behind every major cellular provider in North America.\\n\\nFor fifteen years, corporate telecommunication monopolies kept all of those fees for their corporate executives. But recent federal regulatory mandates established a private consumer restitution framework to route carrier infrastructure fees directly back to verified everyday mobile owners. Everyday citizens like us who pay sixty or seventy dollars a month for cell service while corporate giants profit off our device activity.\\n\\nWhy hasn't this been on the news? I asked. Beverly looked me in the eye and said, Because Big Tech keeps trying to take it down. Every time public presentations explaining this carrier clearinghouse framework get posted online, corporate lawyers scramble to scrub the videos. They want working mothers working convenience store counters for twelve dollars an hour so they can keep their profits private.\\n\\nBeverly tapped her device and sent a private, secure access link directly to my mobile phone. Take this link, Maricela, Beverly said firmly. Go home to your apartment. Make yourself a warm cup of tea, sit down at your table, and watch this presentation from the first minute to the last. Do not skip a single word. And complete the simple three-minute device verification routine tonight before the presentation is taken down.\\n\\nI drove back to Bowman Street in the rain. I turned on the electric kettle, wrapped myself in a blanket, and tapped the link Beverly had sent. A calm, authoritative communications advocate appeared on the screen, speaking plain English without hype. He displayed official regulatory records, demonstrated how carrier micro-transactions are cleared through infrastructure routing pools, and walked viewers through the device verification routine.\\n\\nNo technical coding, no software knowledge, and no business background required. Just a simple three-minute verification routine linking an active mobile line to the daily restitution clearing schedule. My hands shook as I entered my basic device information, confirmed my mobile carrier, and tapped the green verification button. The screen refreshed: Device Verification Complete. Restitution Disbursement Scheduled for Morning Window. It took three minutes and fifteen seconds.\\n\\nI turned off the kitchen light, walked into my small bedroom, and lay down under the quilts. I placed both hands over little Mateo, feeling him move softly in the quiet darkness. I closed my eyes and whispered, Lord, if this is real, please just give me enough to keep my baby warm and safe. I do not need luxury. I just need to be a good mother.\\n\\nAt 6:15 AM, the grey winter dawn was breaking over the rooftops outside. My cell phone gave a sharp, crisp double-vibration on the nightstand. I reached out with trembling fingers, picked up the phone, and swiped open the display. Stamped with an official clearinghouse reference code, a bold notification read: Carrier Clearinghouse Disbursement Confirmed: 428.10. Four hundred twenty-eight dollars and ten cents.\\n\\nFour hundred twenty-eight dollars and ten cents. I stared at the screen until my vision filled with tears. That single morning disbursement was nearly my entire weekly take-home pay for forty-five hours of hauling heavy soda cases and standing on cold concrete. And it had cleared into my mobile balance while I was asleep in bed. Tears poured down my face. God kept His promise. He used a gracious church elder named Beverly to show a broken mother how to claim what corporate monopolies had kept from people like us for decades.\\n\\nAt nine o'clock that morning, I converted the cleared balance into clean paper cash at a local grocery service desk. I bought fresh organic produce, whole milk, chicken, and fresh fruit for the first time in months. The next morning at 6:14 AM, my phone buzzed again: four hundred and twelve dollars and forty cents. The morning after that: three hundred and ninety-six dollars. Within three weeks, over eight thousand dollars in clean cash had accumulated in my hands.\\n\\nI visited a premier baby boutique in north Columbus with cash in my handbag. I purchased a solid cherrywood crib with an organic mattress, a plush nursing rocking chair, a state-of-the-art stroller, twenty cases of infant formula, and three full dressers of soft cotton baby clothes. When the delivery van arrived at my apartment, the drivers carried every single box up the stairs and assembled the furniture with care. I stood in the doorway of Mateo's sunny room with tears of joy, running my fingers over the smooth cherrywood rails.\\n\\nTwo weeks later, my brother Victor hosted a family Sunday dinner at his suburban home. Courtney was in the kitchen, talking loudly with relatives about the church bazaar. I arrived wearing a beautiful tailored maternity coat and walked into the dining room with my head held high. When Courtney saw me looking rested, healthy, and composed, her expression tightened with curiosity.\\n\\nI walked over to the buffet table where Courtney was standing. I reached into my bag, pulled out an envelope containing five crisp one-hundred-dollar bills, and laid it flat on the counter beside her silver platter. I looked Courtney dead in the eye and said, That is for the catering pastries at your baby shower. And you can keep that opened box of pads you dropped in my lap, Courtney. Mateo and I will never need your charity again.\\n\\nCourtney stared at the five hundred dollars in cash, her face turning pale with absolute shock. Victor tried to speak, but I held up my hand and said, I forgive you for what you did in that fellowship hall, Courtney. But you will never look down on my son or me again. I turned and walked out to my car, feeling completely free from the toxic judgment of people who use money to measure human worth.\\n\\nA week later, I went to a local dealership and purchased a pristine, reliable all-wheel-drive crossover in clean cash with zero monthly payments or debt. Mateo will never ride in a rusted clunker with bald tires. He will ride in a safe, warm vehicle with modern safety ratings.\\n\\nToday, Mateo is kicking happily in my belly, ready to arrive in just two weeks. His nursery is completely prepared, our pantry is overflowing with nutritious food, and my apartment lease is paid ahead through the end of next year. I resigned from the gas station night shift last week, dedicating my full time to preparing for my son's birth in peace and dignity. My old manager hugged me and wished me well, surprised that an hourly clerk could leave on her own terms without another job lined up.\\n\\nI am sharing this story today because I know there is another mother reading this right now whose stomach is tied in knots. A woman who has been mocked, dismissed, or judged by family members who think poverty is a character flaw. A mother counting quarters from her purse wondering how she will provide for her baby. You did not fail your child. The corporate system was rigged to keep people like us down, but the door is now open.\\n\\nBeverly warned me, and I will warn you right now: Big Tech keeps trying to take it down. Every time working people begin sharing this carrier clearinghouse presentation, corporate lawyers scramble to pull the link off the web. They do not want ordinary parents discovering how to claim their rightful share of these daily disbursements. If the link below is active when you tap it, do not wait. Watch the presentation and complete the simple three-minute device verification routine. Tap below right now while it is still online.\\n\\nIndividual results may vary. No outcome is guaranteed\",\n          \"title\": \"She Dropped Leftover Pads Onto My Baby Bump\",\n          \"caption\": \"thenationalreviewpost.com\",\n          \"linkUrl\": \"https://c.thenationalreviewpost.com/6ab292a8824bddae485dbb10?sub1={{ad.id}}&sub2=120252732671050695&sub3=120252131444750695&sub4={{ad.name}}&sub5=PP-US_IA_NAT-Batch380-PT380-SB3000-11-03&sub6=PP_LELA4_9-22_ABO-TESTING&sub7={{placement}}&sub8={{site_source_name}}&utm_source=facebook&utm_medium=paid\",\n          \"linkDescription\": \"Link Expires in 2 Hours\",\n          \"callToAction\": \"Learn more\",\n          \"callToActionType\": \"LEARN_MORE\",\n          \"displayFormat\": \"IMAGE\",\n          \"images\": [\n            {\n              \"url\": \"https://scontent-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.35426-6/886655376_1148970560917351_5975047857217583562_n.jpg?_nc_cat=105&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=c53f8f&_nc_ohc=eNXrhjh3D9IQ7kNvwGiCTfH&_nc_oc=AdqUyJZFPUWJo11AdBypP2q9s82-PEAmdo0u1oNTGtx_FeoJ1VuIN3ZdDrHmcE25UW0&_nc_zt=14&_nc_ht=scontent-bos5-1.xx&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&oh=00_AQQ-w2ihloNpc7n93Q_bE54IlMdNH3nsnJlG3GntMF5BwQ&oe=6AF3CA2B\",\n              \"resizedUrl\": \"https://scontent-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.35426-6/888259781_1831864804903140_6836794066472152668_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_s600x600_tt6&_nc_cat=107&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=c53f8f&_nc_ohc=HBZLUSEQoXYQ7kNvwEdUijB&_nc_oc=Adq0QztH7_x3Ku28fjycLqv06uKl-rFVZijBGYZxyAO8ukXAnIZJDcykdKvvH-Xm9Mc&_nc_zt=14&_nc_ht=scontent-bos5-1.xx&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&oh=00_AQTC0YKbbsyYLpuIq5tcq8Z94kmhYiBhixsvYwFL3FOtKw&oe=6AF3BEAB\",\n              \"watermarkedResizedUrl\": null\n            }\n          ],\n          \"videos\": [],\n          \"cards\": []\n        },\n        \"brandedContent\": null,\n        \"euTransparency\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"2191529528384363\",\n        \"adId\": null,\n        \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/ads/library?id=2191529528384363\",\n        \"page\": {\n          \"id\": \"990707490939476\",\n          \"name\": \"Richie Contartesi\",\n          \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/RelentlessRichie/\",\n          \"isDeleted\": false,\n          \"profilePictureUrl\": \"https://scontent-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.35426-6/858780101_1650334693166499_4659195662810189417_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_s60x60_tt6&_nc_cat=110&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=c53f8f&_nc_ohc=jym4P-ctEtcQ7kNvwFcyrpG&_nc_oc=AdqDxYDKE773EMFx-XugzHWmLRHFDFCCZ1VfiMAy5NwnCcJymbGYscjc1unuBi5Y1Wc&_nc_zt=14&_nc_ht=scontent-bos5-1.xx&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&oh=00_AQSucpRmPeqDEiD8o1HguvkxBE8V_dcEFVPr-O9byF19og&oe=6AF3C5DF\",\n          \"likeCount\": 10107,\n          \"categories\": [\n            \"Entrepreneur\"\n          ]\n        },\n        \"campaign\": {\n          \"collationId\": \"4443194255960009\",\n          \"collationCount\": 2\n        },\n        \"isActive\": true,\n        \"publisherPlatforms\": [\n          \"FACEBOOK\",\n          \"INSTAGRAM\",\n          \"AUDIENCE_NETWORK\",\n          \"THREADS\"\n        ],\n        \"startedAt\": \"2026-10-23T07:00:00.000Z\",\n        \"endedAt\": \"2026-11-06T08:00:00.000Z\",\n        \"categories\": [\n          \"UNKNOWN\"\n        ],\n        \"countries\": [],\n        \"impressions\": {\n          \"text\": null,\n          \"index\": -1\n        },\n        \"spend\": null,\n        \"creative\": {\n          \"bodyText\": \"Parents of High School Football Players in the 2027/28 Classes:\\n\\nYou've been telling your son to post on social, but he is not.\\n\\nYou've been telling your son to do outreach to college football coaches, but he is not.\\n\\nAnd you don't have thousands of hours to learn the recruiting process…\\n\\nPlus, you're probably already paying for personal trainers, private schools, and maybe even a nutritionist because you don't want to let your son settle for average. \\n\\nBut he's still not getting the level of exposure he deserves and you have no idea where to start.\\n\\nWell, if that's you, Then keep reading…\\n\\nI'm looking for a few more highly motivated families to work with 1-on-1 who are tired of the endless cycle of camps, recruiting services, and frustrating “recruiting softwares”…\\n\\nAnd who want to invest in mentorship that will transform their son into a confident communicator and marketer.\\n\\nAs a high-performing leader yourself, you don’t settle for average in your work, and you don’t want your son to settle for average either.\\n\\nYour son is already an extremely hard worker on the field and in the weight room, but he is either shy or doesn’t understand that college football recruiting is now a business…\\n\\nThis means he also must develop the skills to properly market and sell himself…\\n\\nAs well as build unshakable confidence in himself to build real relationships with college coaches...\\n\\nBecause outside of just being a good player, that’s what it takes to earn a scholarship in the new NIL/Portal era.\\n\\nIt’s no secret that doing outreach yourself is free…But the protocol I teach is reserved for those who value their time…\\n\\nAnd want the protocols normally reserved for the top 1% of high school football players.\\n\\nThe Next Play® System is expensive, exclusive, and wildly effective.\\n\\nIn fact, not only did I earn a D1 football scholarship at Ole Miss, despite being just 5’7 and 150 lbs (and no, not a kicker 😅)…\\n\\nBut 94% of my athletes have earned scholarships worth $100,000 - $200,000, with 42% going Division 1.\\n\\nHow? While others waste time at camps without relationships built before attending, sending generic emails through recruiting software, or just seeking “exposure”...\\n\\nI focus on transforming driven high school football players into confident leaders, communicators, and businessmen…\\n\\nGiving them a step-by-step scholarship system to follow and enabling them to build relationships with college coaches, leading to scholarship offers.\\n\\nThe entire protocol for earning a scholarship, the college football business, leadership, and mental toughness, are all addressed.\\n\\nDo exactly what we say, and you can watch your son run out of the tunnel in front of 100,000 screaming fans, then post about it for the world to see.\\n\\nThis way, like my parents, you can prove all his haters wrong and laugh as you listen to other parents blame the portal and every other made-up excuse besides doing what actually works.\\n\\nClick the \\\"learn more\\\" button to see if it's the right fit.\\n\\n~ Richie Contartesi\\n\\nP.S. This is not a recruiting service, so if you don’t understand the value of mentorship, then please keep scrolling.\",\n          \"title\": \"Earn a College Football Scholarship 🏈\",\n          \"caption\": \"gonextplay.com\",\n          \"linkUrl\": \"https://gonextplay.com/application-ads\",\n          \"linkDescription\": null,\n          \"callToAction\": \"Learn more\",\n          \"callToActionType\": \"LEARN_MORE\",\n          \"displayFormat\": \"VIDEO\",\n          \"images\": [],\n          \"videos\": [\n            {\n              \"hdUrl\": \"https://video-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/o1/v/t2/f2/m366/AQNIxzbybTMNZF92yJ_2X_c-7HXj7pEy9-noTo-HDG-NlU3mRmMfzGyTcY0VUhElydgXlC8ZkXgO-gK3I-NKH0ZmdhXg8846sV8B0E7Hg_0s2g.mp4?_nc_cat=105&_nc_sid=b66105&_nc_ht=video-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net&_nc_ohc=UwLxTcEycosQ7kNvwE9fp2f&efg=eyJ2ZW5jb2RlX3RhZyI6Inhwdl9wcm9ncmVzc2l2ZS5WSV9VU0VDQVNFX1BST0RVQ1RfVFlQRS4uQzMuNzIwLmRhc2hfaDI2NC1iYXNpYy1nZW4yXzcyMHAiLCJ4cHZfYXNzZXRfaWQiOjEzOTAyODQzOTU4MjUyMTcsImFzc2V0X2FnZV9kYXlzIjozMzIsInZpX3VzZWNhc2VfaWQiOjEwNzk5LCJkdXJhdGlvbl9zIjo5OCwidXJsZ2VuX3NvdXJjZSI6Ind3dyJ9&ccb=17-1&vs=f9adb4d2ea2cd547&_nc_&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&_nc_zt=28&oh=00_AQSXD1TBrX9LRmr37drGXiealTSSMwh5TmkirPERaRZZwg&oe=6AF3CCB3\",\n              \"sdUrl\": \"https://video-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/o1/v/t2/f2/m412/AQP5DSTvmXKUSK4J2KEjf1On4-w8nWJxxy86oa-WLkGR5Fq2duV0HGDZiPC9Xz4e91hxcc8lF7cvpfXXMYwH7vbbxIXq2XNkZULCHoaeEw.mp4?_nc_cat=105&_nc_sid=ef5aa3&_nc_ht=video-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net&_nc_ohc=PHaIbxnOlbUQ7kNvwGyuAhR&efg=eyJ2ZW5jb2RlX3RhZyI6Inhwdl9wcm9ncmVzc2l2ZS5WSV9VU0VDQVNFX1BST0RVQ1RfVFlQRS4uQzMuMzYwLnN2ZV9zZCIsInhwdl9hc3NldF9pZCI6MTM5MDI4NDM5NTgyNTIxNywiYXNzZXRfYWdlX2RheXMiOjMzMiwidmlfdXNlY2FzZV9pZCI6MTA3OTksImR1cmF0aW9uX3MiOjk4LCJ1cmxnZW5fc291cmNlIjoid3d3In0%3D&ccb=17-1&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&_nc_zt=28&oh=00_AQTemUZJa7CYK1oePP0Q81jJ52nOqOmvi0RWoKpL_3VEeA&oe=6AF3ECD9\",\n              \"previewImageUrl\": \"https://scontent-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.35426-6/864244866_1111696097910088_7248430230690751962_n.jpg?_nc_cat=103&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=c53f8f&_nc_ohc=5lGM3eDKuWgQ7kNvwGzqoGS&_nc_oc=AdqZm6rh1XZiVRZPbfTonwR_XPWwe2qyLASEeSl5uuygnMU6u-BS06EIF-l3B15uk2o&_nc_zt=14&_nc_ht=scontent-bos5-1.xx&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&oh=00_AQRwRIc_xb9ywP5Rg6cIZ4ipK31dUeETpJP-C2jvvHznRQ&oe=6AF3E4B0\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"cards\": []\n        },\n        \"brandedContent\": null,\n        \"euTransparency\": null\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"2280780462489222\",\n        \"adId\": null,\n        \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/ads/library?id=2280780462489222\",\n        \"page\": {\n          \"id\": \"990707490939476\",\n          \"name\": \"Richie Contartesi\",\n          \"url\": \"https://www.facebook.com/RelentlessRichie/\",\n          \"isDeleted\": false,\n          \"profilePictureUrl\": \"https://scontent-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.35426-6/858459928_1148648667821187_1288374074601829116_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_s60x60_tt6&_nc_cat=101&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=c53f8f&_nc_ohc=xxFphA_9usoQ7kNvwEv8_O_&_nc_oc=AdqxtARWD8MtNKgfp-YTeIvaBrqdr1qBsgHFsesxvlkKl1ootfjVG8OcggQr9VkTAiQ&_nc_zt=14&_nc_ht=scontent-bos5-1.xx&_nc_gid=OhCQXAk4Jx5TMYnIZqFTCg&_nc_ss=78180&oh=00_AQSAaXBvUE6NrVYvdy1B9T2Vn8XcJfHOsmuQp821ZpzdRw&oe=6AF3CA25\",\n          \"likeCount\": 10107,\n          \"categories\": [\n            \"Entrepreneur\"\n          ]\n        },\n        \"campaign\": {\n          \"collationId\": \"1698149541317097\",\n          \"collationCount\": 2\n        },\n        \"isActive\": true,\n        \"publisherPlatforms\": [\n          \"FACEBOOK\",\n          \"INSTAGRAM\",\n          \"AUDIENCE_NETWORK\",\n          \"THREADS\"\n        ],\n        \"startedAt\": \"2026-10-23T07:00:00.000Z\",\n        \"endedAt\": \"2026-11-06T08:00:00.000Z\",\n        \"categories\": [\n          \"UNKNOWN\"\n        ],\n        \"countries\": [],\n        \"impressions\": {\n          \"text\": null,\n          \"index\": -1\n        },\n        \"spend\": null,\n        \"creative\": {\n          \"bodyText\": \"Parents of High School Football Players in the 2027/28 Classes:\\n\\nYou've been telling your son to post on social, but he is not.\\n\\nYou've been telling your son to do outreach to college football coaches, but he is not.\\n\\nAnd you don't have thousands of hours to learn the recruiting process…\\n\\nPlus, you're probably already paying for personal trainers, private schools, and maybe even a nutritionist because you don't want to let your son settle for average. \\n\\nBut he's still not getting the level of exposure he deserves and you have no idea where to start.\\n\\nWell, if that's you, Then keep reading…\\n\\nI'm looking for a few more highly motivated families to work with 1-on-1 who are tired of the endless cycle of camps, recruiting services, and frustrating “recruiting softwares”…\\n\\nAnd who want to invest in mentorship that will transform their son into a confident communicator and marketer.\\n\\nAs a high-performing leader yourself, you don’t settle for average in your work, and you don’t want your son to settle for average either.\\n\\nYour son is already an extremely hard worker on the field and in the weight room, but he is either shy or doesn’t understand that college football recruiting is now a business…\\n\\nThis means he also must develop the skills to properly market and sell himself…\\n\\nAs well as build unshakable confidence in himself to build real relationships with college coaches...\\n\\nBecause outside of just being a good player, that’s what it takes to earn a scholarship in the new NIL/Portal era.\\n\\nIt’s no secret that doing outreach yourself is free…But the protocol I teach is reserved for those who value their time…\\n\\nAnd want the protocols normally reserved for the top 1% of high school football players.\\n\\nThe Next Play® System is expensive, exclusive, and wildly effective.\\n\\nIn fact, not only did I earn a D1 football scholarship at Ole Miss, despite being just 5’7 and 150 lbs (and no, not a kicker 😅)…\\n\\nBut 94% of my athletes have earned scholarships worth $100,000 - $200,000, with 42% going Division 1.\\n\\nHow? While others waste time at camps without relationships built before attending, sending generic emails through recruiting software, or just seeking “exposure”...\\n\\nI focus on transforming driven high school football players into confident leaders, communicators, and businessmen…\\n\\nGiving them a step-by-step scholarship system to follow and enabling them to build relationships with college coaches, leading to scholarship offers.\\n\\nThe entire protocol for earning a scholarship, the college football business, leadership, and mental toughness, are all addressed.\\n\\nDo exactly what we say, and you can watch your son run out of the tunnel in front of 100,000 screaming fans, then post about it for the world to see.\\n\\nThis way, like my parents, you can prove all his haters wrong and laugh as you listen to other parents blame the portal and every other made-up excuse besides doing what actually works.\\n\\nClick the \\\"learn more\\\" button to see if it's the right fit.\\n\\n~ Richie Contartesi\\n\\nP.S. This is not a recruiting service, so if you don’t understand the value of mentorship, then please keep scrolling.\",\n          \"title\": \"Earn a College Football Scholarship 🏈\",\n          \"caption\": \"gonextplay.com\",\n          \"linkUrl\": \"https://gonextplay.com/application-ads\",\n          \"linkDescription\": null,\n          \"callToAction\": \"Learn more\",\n          \"callToActionType\": \"LEARN_MORE\",\n          \"displayFormat\": \"VIDEO\",\n          \"images\": [],\n          \"videos\": [\n            {\n              \"hdUrl\": \"https://video-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net/o1/v/t2/f2/m366/AQN7NYoWhucAAVU15qlRgaRiQHblo5dorLRtkznt29quzw6YvYeg-LKHPmL5Jxl-HSuNB8-W-Wlnt7m8qvjVoduX3hmIvJzrh-qWlL7ffYQFrg.mp4?_nc_cat=104&_nc_sid=b66105&_nc_ht=video-bos5-1.xx.fbcdn.net&_nc_ohc